\section{Introduction}\label{sec:code-layout-intro}

\emph{Code layout optimization} determines the placement of basic blocks and functions in the final executable. While seemingly a low-level concern, code layout has a profound impact on performance: it affects instruction cache utilization, branch prediction accuracy, and instruction fetch bandwidth. A well-laid-out program can be 10--20\% faster than the same program with a poor layout, even when the instruction sequences are identical.

This chapter covers the major code layout techniques: basic block placement, trace formation, cache-aware layout, branch prediction optimization, hot/cold splitting, and profile-guided layout using the Pettis-Hansen algorithm.

\section{Basic Block Placement}\label{sec:basic-block-placement}

\subsection{The Problem}\label{sec:bbp-problem}

\begin{definition}[Basic Block Placement Problem]\label{def:bbp}
Given a CFG with basic blocks and edge probabilities (from profiling or heuristics), find a linear ordering of the blocks that minimizes the expected number of taken branches (or maximizes instruction cache utilization).
\end{definition}

The basic block placement problem is NP-hard in general (it generalizes the traveling salesman problem), but effective heuristics exist.

\subsection{The Pettis-Hansen Algorithm}\label{sec:pettis-hansen}

The \emph{Pettis-Hansen algorithm}~\cite{pettis1990profile} (1990) is the most widely used block placement heuristic. It constructs \emph{traces}---linear sequences of blocks that are likely to execute consecutively---and places them contiguously in memory.

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Pettis-Hansen Block Placement}
\label{alg:pettis-hansen}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require CFG with edge probabilities
\Ensure Linear ordering of basic blocks
\State Sort all edges by probability (highest first)
\State $\mathit{traces} \gets \emptyset$
\ForAll{edges $e = (u, v)$ in decreasing probability order}
  \If{$u$ is not the end of a trace and $v$ is not the start of a trace}
    \State Merge the traces containing $u$ and $v$
  \ElsIf{$u$ is the end of a trace and $v$ is not in any trace}
    \State Append $v$ to $u$'s trace
  \ElsIf{$v$ is the start of a trace and $u$ is not in any trace}
    \State Prepend $u$ to $v$'s trace
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\State Order traces by their entry block's execution frequency
\State Place traces contiguously in memory
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Properties}\label{sec:pettis-properties}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Fall-through optimization}: On architectures where branches are more expensive than fall-through (e.g., x86), placing the likely successor immediately after the block eliminates a branch.
  \item \textbf{Cache locality}: Blocks in the same trace are placed contiguously, improving instruction cache locality for the hot path.
  \item \textbf{Branch prediction}: The processor's branch predictor learns the fall-through pattern, improving prediction accuracy.
\end{itemize}

\section{Trace Formation}\label{sec:trace-formation}

\subsection{What is a Trace?}\label{sec:trace-def}

\begin{definition}[Trace]\label{def:trace}
A \textbf{trace} is a linear sequence of basic blocks $B_1, B_2, \ldots, B_k$ such that the most likely execution path passes through these blocks in order. Traces represent the ``hot path'' through the program.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Trace Selection Algorithms}\label{sec:trace-selection}

\begin{definition}[Trace Selection]\label{def:trace-selection}
\textbf{Trace selection} identifies the most important traces in a program. Methods include:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Frequency-based}: Start from the most frequently executed block and follow the most likely successors.
  \item \textbf{Loop-based}: Each loop body is a trace, with the loop header as the entry.
  \item \textbf{Path-based}: Use profiling to identify the most common execution paths.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Trace Formation}
\label{alg:trace-formation}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require CFG with edge probabilities
\Ensure Set of traces covering all blocks
\State $\mathit{unplaced} \gets$ all blocks
\While{$\mathit{unplaced} \neq \emptyset$}
  \State $B \gets$ block in $\mathit{unplaced}$ with highest execution frequency
  \State $\mathit{trace} \gets [B]$
  \State Remove $B$ from $\mathit{unplaced}$
  \State $\mathit{current} \gets B$
  \While{true}
    \State $s \gets$ most likely successor of $\mathit{current}$ that is in $\mathit{unplaced}$
    \If{$s$ exists and $P(\mathit{current} \to s) > \mathit{threshold}$}
      \State Append $s$ to $\mathit{trace}$
      \State Remove $s$ from $\mathit{unplaced}$
      \State $\mathit{current} \gets s$
    \Else
      \State Break
    \EndIf
  \EndWhile
  \State Add $\mathit{trace}$ to $\mathit{traces}$
\EndWhile
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Cache-Aware Code Layout}\label{sec:cache-aware}

\subsection{Instruction Cache Effects}\label{sec:icache-effects}

The instruction cache (I-cache) stores recently fetched instructions. When the processor needs an instruction not in the cache, it incurs a \emph{cache miss} penalty (typically 10--100 cycles). Code layout affects I-cache performance in two ways:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Spatial locality}: Blocks placed close together in memory are likely to be in the same cache line.
  \item \textbf{Temporal locality}: Frequently executed blocks stay in the cache.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Cache-Aware Placement}\label{sec:cache-aware-placement}

\begin{definition}[Cache-Aware Placement]\label{def:cache-aware}
\textbf{Cache-aware placement} considers the cache geometry (line size, associativity, total size) when laying out code. The goal is to minimize cache misses by:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Keeping hot code within the cache.
  \item Avoiding \emph{cache thrashing}: multiple hot blocks mapping to the same cache set.
  \item Aligning hot blocks to cache line boundaries.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Cache Simulation}\label{sec:cache-simulation}

Cache-aware layout tools often include a \emph{cache simulator} that estimates the I-cache miss rate for a given layout. The layout optimizer iterates, trying different placements and selecting the one with the lowest estimated miss rate.

\section{Branch Prediction Optimization}\label{sec:branch-prediction}

\subsection{Static Branch Prediction}\label{sec:static-prediction}

\begin{definition}[Static Branch Prediction]\label{def:static-pred}
\textbf{Static branch prediction} uses compile-time information to predict branch outcomes. Common heuristics:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Backward branches (loops) are predicted taken.
  \item Forward branches are predicted not taken.
  \item Branches to frequently executed targets are predicted taken.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Branch Layout Optimization}\label{sec:branch-layout}

The layout of branches affects prediction accuracy:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Branch shortening}: Replace long branches with short branches when the target is nearby.
  \item \textbf{Branch inversion}: Invert the branch condition so the likely path falls through.
  \item \textbf{Branch elimination}: Remove branches that are always taken or never taken (from profiling).
\end{itemize}

\section{Hot/Cold Splitting}\label{sec:hot-cold-splitting}

\subsection{Overview}\label{sec:hot-cold-overview}

\emph{Hot/cold splitting} separates frequently executed code (``hot'') from rarely executed code (``cold'') and places them in different sections of the executable.

\begin{definition}[Hot/Cold Splitting]\label{def:hot-cold}
\textbf{Hot/cold splitting} divides the program into:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Hot section}: Contains frequently executed code. Placed to maximize cache utilization and minimize branch penalties.
  \item \textbf{Cold section}: Contains rarely executed code (error handlers, unusual paths). Placed at the end of the executable.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Benefits}\label{sec:hot-cold-benefits}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Cache efficiency}: The hot section fits in the I-cache; cold code does not displace hot code.
  \item \textbf{Page faults}: Cold code can be placed on pages that are not loaded until needed.
  \item \textbf{Code size}: The hot section is smaller, improving fetch bandwidth.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Implementation}\label{sec:hot-cold-impl}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Hot/Cold Splitting}
\label{alg:hot-cold}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Program with profile data
\Ensure Split hot and cold code
\State Compute execution frequency for each basic block
\State $\mathit{hot\_threshold} \gets$ frequency cutoff (e.g., top 90\% of execution)
\ForAll{basic blocks $B$}
  \If{$\mathit{freq}(B) \geq \mathit{hot\_threshold}$}
    \State Mark $B$ as hot
  \Else
    \State Mark $B$ as cold
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\State Place hot blocks first (using Pettis-Hansen)
\State Place cold blocks after hot blocks
\State Insert jumps from hot to cold blocks where needed
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Profile-Guided Layout (Pettis-Hansen)}\label{sec:pgl}

\subsection{Profile Collection}\label{sec:profile-collection}

Profile-guided layout requires execution counts for basic blocks or edges. These are collected by:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Instrumentation}: Compile with profiling code that counts block executions.
  \item \textbf{Hardware profiling}: Use processor performance counters (e.g., Intel LBR, ARM SPE).
  \item \textbf{Sampling}: Periodically sample the program counter.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The Pettis-Hansen Algorithm (Detailed)}\label{sec:pettis-detailed}

The Pettis-Hansen algorithm (Algorithm~\ref{alg:pettis-hansen}) works as follows:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Sort edges}: All CFG edges are sorted by their execution probability (or count), in decreasing order.
  \item \textbf{Build traces}: Process edges in order. For each edge $(u, v)$:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item If $u$ and $v$ are in different traces, merge them.
      \item If $u$ is at the end of a trace and $v$ is unplaced, extend the trace.
      \item If $v$ is at the start of a trace and $u$ is unplaced, prepend to the trace.
    \end{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Order traces}: Sort traces by the execution frequency of their entry block.
  \item \textbf{Place blocks}: Place traces contiguously in memory, most frequent first.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}[Pettis-Hansen Example]\label{ex:pettis}
Consider a program with the following edge frequencies:
\begin{verbatim}
  A → B: 1000   B → C: 900   C → D: 800
  A → E: 100    E → F: 100   D → A: 700 (back edge)
\end{verbatim}
Sorted edges: A→B (1000), B→C (900), C→D (800), D→A (700), A→E (100), E→F (100).
Trace formation: A-B-C-D (following the hot path). Then E-F as a separate trace.
Layout: [A, B, C, D] [E, F]
\end{example}

\begin{historical}
The Pettis-Hansen algorithm was published in 1990~\cite{pettis1990profile} and remains the standard block placement algorithm used in production compilers. Hot/cold splitting was introduced by Calder et al.~\cite{calder1997online} (1997) and is now a standard feature in GCC and LLVM. Cache-aware layout was developed by Johnson et al.~\cite{johnson1993branch} (1993) and others. Modern work on code layout includes the Bolt tool from Facebook~\cite{novillo2019bolt} (2019), which performs post-link optimization of code layout using profile data.
\end{historical}

