\section{Relaxing Branches After Blocks Move}

A frequency-based layout assigns an order, not a final address. An out-of-range branch may need a longer encoding, which in turn moves other branches and their targets. It is not enough to visit each branch once using offsets from the initial layout.

\textbf{Inputs and assumptions.} Fix the block order and suppose each direct branch has a short form and a longer form with equivalent effects. The long form reaches every relevant target in this simplified model and can be used without changing register state. The example omits alignment and uses displacement relative to the end of the branch. Real targets can define a different displacement base.

\begin{sudocode}[Widen encodings until the layout fits]
\Require Ordered blocks and branch encodings
\Ensure Every branch is encodable at its final address
\State Initialize every relaxable branch to short
\Repeat
  \Let{A}{\Call{AssignAddresses}{blocks, encodings}}
  \Let{W}{\emptyset}
  \ForAll{branches $b$ still short}
    \Let{d}{A[\mathrm{target}(b)]-\Call{EndAddress}{A,b}}
    \If{$d$ does not fit the short form}
      \State Add $b$ to $W$ \Comment{Use a consistent address map}
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
  \State Set the encodings in $W$ to long
  \State \Comment{Never undo a widening in this pass}
\Until{$W=\emptyset$}
\State \Return $A$ and the encodings
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Example.} Let short branches be two bytes and allow forward displacements up to 127; long branches are five bytes. Branches $A$ and $B$ start at offsets 0 and 10, and their targets are at 129 and 160. The displacements are 127 and 148. Widening $B$ moves both targets three bytes forward. Now $A$ needs displacement 130 and must widen on the next pass. The final target offsets are 135 and 166.

\textbf{Failure and cost.} Each nonfinal pass widens at least one branch; there are at most $b+1$ address maps for $b$ candidates. The stable map is safe even if a more elaborate layout could shorten a previously widened branch. If the long form can also overflow, this procedure needs a separate remedy, such as a target-specific range extension, or must report failure.

\section{Summary}\label{sec:code-layout-summary}

Code layout optimization determines the physical placement of code in the executable. The Pettis-Hansen algorithm uses profile data to place hot code contiguously, improving cache utilization and branch prediction. Trace formation identifies the most likely execution paths. Cache-aware layout considers the I-cache geometry. Hot/cold splitting separates frequently and rarely executed code. These optimizations are particularly important for large programs where instruction cache pressure is significant. Profile-guided optimization (Chapter~\ref{sec:pgo}) provides the execution data needed for effective layout decisions.

\section{Trace Formation Algorithms}\label{sec:trace-formation}

\emph{Trace formation} identifies the most frequently executed paths through the program and lays them out contiguously in memory.

\begin{definition}[Trace]\label{def:trace}
A \textbf{trace} is a sequence of basic blocks that forms a likely execution path. Formally, a trace is a maximal single-entry, single-exit (SESE) region where the entry edge has the highest probability among all incoming edges.
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Trace Formation Algorithm}
\label{alg:trace-formation}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require CFG with edge probabilities, unplaced blocks $U$
\ensure Set of traces covering all blocks
\State $traces \gets \emptyset$
\While{$U \neq \emptyset$}
  \State Select unplaced block $b$ with highest execution frequency
  \State $T \gets [b]$ \Comment{Start new trace}
  \State $current \gets b$
  \While{true}
    \State Find most likely successor $s$ of $current$
    \If{$s$ is unplaced and $s$ has no other placed predecessor}
      \State Append $s$ to $T$
      \State Remove $s$ from $U$
      \State $current \gets s$
    \Else
      \State \textbf{break}
    \EndIf
  \EndWhile
  \State Add $T$ to $traces$
  \State Remove blocks of $T$ from $U$
\EndWhile
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Extended Traces}\label{sec:extended-traces}

\begin{definition}[Extended Trace]\label{def:extended-trace}
An \textbf{extended trace} allows loops to be included in the trace by following back edges. This captures the common case of loop bodies being executed many times.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Non-Sequential Trace]\label{def:non-seq-trace}
A \textbf{non-sequential trace} allows the trace to include blocks that are not immediate successors, connected by low-probability edges. This captures paths that skip over unlikely branches.
\end{definition}

\section{Cache-Aware Code Layout}\label{sec:cache-aware-layout}

\emph{Cache-aware} code layout considers the geometry of the instruction cache when placing code.

\begin{definition}[I-Cache Conflict Miss]\label{def:icache-conflict}
An \textbf{I-cache conflict miss} occurs when two frequently executed code blocks map to the same cache set, causing one to evict the other. This is common when code blocks are separated by a distance that is a multiple of the cache set size.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cache-Aware Placement]\label{def:cache-aware-placement}
\textbf{Cache-aware placement} assigns each basic block to a physical address such that:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Hot blocks are placed contiguously (spatial locality).
  \item Blocks that execute together do not map to the same cache set (avoiding conflict misses).
  \item The total working set of hot code fits in the I-cache.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Cache-Aware Block Placement}
\label{alg:cache-aware}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Traces $T_1, \ldots, T_k$ with execution frequencies, cache geometry
\ensure Physical addresses for all blocks
\State $addr \gets 0$
\ForAll{traces $T_i$ in frequency order (hottest first)}
  \ForAll{blocks $b \in T_i$}
    \State $set(b) \gets addr \mod C_{set\_count}$
    \If{$set(b)$ conflicts with a hot block already placed}
      \State Insert NOP padding to shift $set(b)$
    \EndIf
    \State $address(b) \gets addr$
    \State $addr \gets addr + size(b)$
    \State Align $addr$ to instruction boundary
  \EndFor
\EndFor
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{example}[Cache Conflict Resolution]\label{ex:cache-conflict}
\begin{verbatim}
  // I-cache: 32KB, 8-way set associative, 64-byte lines
  // Number of sets: 32KB / (8 × 64) = 64 sets
  
  // Problem: hot_loop_A at address 0x1000 and hot_loop_B at 0x1040
  // Both map to set 0 (0x1000 / 64 = 64, 64 mod 64 = 0)
  // They conflict! Each evicts the other.
  
  // Solution: insert NOP padding between them
  // hot_loop_B moved to 0x1080 → set 1 (no conflict)
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\section{Hot/Cold Splitting}\label{sec:hot-cold-splitting}

\begin{definition}[Hot/Cold Splitting]\label{def:hot-cold-split}
\textbf{Hot/cold splitting} separates a function's code into:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Hot section}: Frequently executed code (main path, loop bodies).
  \item \textbf{Cold section}: Rarely executed code (error handlers, unlikely branches).
\end{itemize}
The cold section is placed far from the hot section, reducing I-cache pressure.
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Hot/Cold Splitting}
\label{alg:hot-cold-split}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Function $f$ with profile data, threshold $\tau$
\ensure Split function with hot and cold sections
\State Identify hot blocks: $H = \{b \mid freq(b) \geq \tau \cdot freq(entry)\}$
\State Identify cold blocks: $C = \{b \mid b \notin H\}$
\State Place $H$ contiguously in the hot text section
\State Place $C$ in the cold text section (separate ELF section)
\ForAll{edges from $H$ to $C$}
  \State Insert long branch to cold section
\EndFor
\ForAll{edges from $C$ to $H$}
  \State Insert long branch back to hot section
\EndFor
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

